<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation"
xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation
https://opencfs.gitlab.io/cfs/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd">

  <documentation>
    <title>Single load feature mapping compliance with the complete exact Hessian from C++, driven by native IPOPT</title>
    <authors>
      <author>Fabian Wein</author>
    </authors>
    <date>2026-07-30</date>
    <references>no</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      Cantilever compliance minimization with a single tip load on a feature mapping design of two
      pills, optimized by the built-in IPOPT with the exact Lagrangian Hessian - no embedded python
      involved, hence this is the only Hessian test running in a build without USE_EMBEDDED_PYTHON.

      hessian="exact" in the ipopt element makes cfs serve IPOPT::eval_h() with
      obj_factor * Hess(f) + sum_c lambda_c Hess(c_c) from BaseOptimizer::CalcObjectiveHessian() and
      CalcConstraintHessian() - the same assembly the python drivers get via cfs.evalhessian and
      cfs.evalhessian_constr. For the NATIVE compliance objective that is the geometric part
      (FeatureMappingDesign::CalcShapeHessian with the zero rho-curvature of the identity transfer
      function) plus the dense state-curvature block 2 B^T Z (ErsatzMaterial::CalcStateHessianBlock:
      the pseudo loads b_i = dK/ds_i u and n back-substitutions against the factorized state matrix).
      The volume constraint contributes its exact Hessian, hence 'Number of Lagrangian Hessian
      evaluations' in the IPOPT log is the test content, next to the converged design.

      The finite difference assertion of this Hessian lives in hessian_multiload_compliance
      (fd_check="true" via hessian_scipy.py). IPOPT's own 'derivative_test' option checks eval_h too,
      but it perturbs the design behind cfs' back and thus disturbs the objective autoscaling - use it
      only manually for diagnosis, not in a test.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <regular geometryType="plane" region="mech">
        <box xMax="1.0" yMax="1.0"/>
        <elements nx="12"/>
      </regular>
    </input>
    <output>
      <hdf5/>
      <info/>
    </output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="plane">
    <regionList>
      <region material="99lines" name="mech"/>
    </regionList>
  </domain>

  <sequenceStep index="1">
    <analysis>
      <static/>
    </analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="planeStress">
        <regionList>
          <region name="mech"/>
        </regionList>

        <!-- cantilever: clamped left edge, tip load at the lower right corner -->
        <bcsAndLoads>
           <fix name="left">
              <comp dof="x"/>
              <comp dof="y"/>
           </fix>
           <force name="bottom_right">
             <comp dof="y" value="-1"/>
           </force>
        </bcsAndLoads>
        <storeResults>
          <elemResult type="mechPseudoDensity">
            <allRegions/>
          </elemResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>
  </sequenceStep>

  <optimization>
    <costFunction type="compliance" task="minimize"/>

    <!-- native volume constraint; linear="false" is the optimizer semantics (non-linear in the
         shape variables), the exact Hessian comes from the zero rho-curvature of linear functions -->
    <constraint type="volume" bound="upperBound" value="0.35" linear="false"/>

    <!-- native IPOPT with the exact Lagrangian Hessian from C++ (IPOPT::eval_h), alternative python  with hessian_ipopt.py -->
    <optimizer type="ipopt" maxIterations="5">
      <ipopt hessian="exact">
        <option type="real" name="tol" value="1e-4"/>
      </ipopt>
    </optimizer>

    <!-- boundary is the C^2 quintic; the exact geometric Hessian needs H'' -->
    <ersatzMaterial region="mech" material="mechanic" method="featureMapping">
      <featureMapping transition="0.1" boundary="quintic" order="3" combine="p-norm" p="3" hessexport="true">
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.3"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.0"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.9"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.1" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
      </featureMapping>
      <design name="density" initial=".5" physical_lower="1e-3" upper="1.0"/>

      <transferFunction type="identity" application="mech"/>
      <export save="all" write="iteration"/>
    </ersatzMaterial>
    <commit mode="forward" stride="1"/>
  </optimization>
</cfsSimulation>
